# Coleman power series, part II: target signatures

The roadmap is README.md; this file is not the roadmap. It records the roadmap's
headline theorems against the pinned APIs.

The following headline theorems are not statable at the pins:

- Two-variable local-unit freeness and anticyclotomic rank two: The quadratic Lubin–Tate division tower, its norm-compatible principal-unit O-module and its actual completed Galois action are not carriers at the pins.

- Height-two Lubin–Tate Coleman interpolation: The Lubin–Tate formal O-module with marked Tate basis, its analytic torsion evaluation and its arithmetic norm tower are missing.

- Perfect formal Kummer duality and Wiles reciprocity: The Lubin–Tate torsion realization, formal-norm tower and the Kummer/Artin pairing on that tower are missing.

- Signed annihilator theorem: The actual character derivatives on the anticyclotomic unit quotient and the signed formal-point carriers are missing.

- Existence of an optimal plus unit: The signed anticyclotomic unit module and its normalized Coates–Wiles augmentation derivative are missing.

- Existence of the primitive formal-CM local-point trace system: Marked quasi-canonical elliptic quotients and their local ring-class fields, the modular-image system and its formal comparison are missing.

- Minus divisible-point invariants and minus quotient/kernel freeness: The signed formal-point module, actual tower action and signed unit quotient are missing.

- Rubin’s integral decomposition and signed rank-one freeness: The actual inverse-twisted anticyclotomic local-unit quotient and its two derivative-kernel submodules are missing.

- Nonvanishing of every matching-parity derivative of every signed generator: The signed unit module and its character Coates–Wiles derivatives are missing.

Native power series, elliptic formal points and completed group algebras do not
supply these missing arithmetic constructions. Constructing the marked tower or
the signed unit module is itself library work. No arbitrary-module substitute or
free predicate is used in place of a headline theorem.
